  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Bleeding Oak Crust is a thin, crustose fungus that forms flat or slightly raised patches on dead hardwood, particularly oak. It is named for its distinctive reddish coloration that can appear to weep or bleed when wet.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Reddish-brown coloration",
      "description": "The fruiting body displays a characteristic rust or blood-red color, especially visible when moist.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Crustose form",
      "description": "Grows as a thin, tightly adherent crust that closely follows the contours of the wood surface without forming shelf-like structures.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth to slightly bumpy underside",
      "description": "The hymenial surface (spore-bearing side) is smooth or finely wrinkled, distinguishing it from related polypore species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Bleeding Oak Crust is found on dead or dying hardwood logs and branches, particularly oak, in moist forests and woodlands throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its name suggesting active bleeding, the reddish pigmentation is a fungal compound that becomes more vivid when the wood absorbs moisture, creating the illusion of fresh fluid seeping from the wood.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
